BATTERY_CONTEXT = '''<h2>The challenge and why simulate it</h2><p>Battery cooling must move heat from the module interior through interfaces and into the coolant. Low through-plane conductivity, local heat generation, cold-plate coverage and coolant warming can produce temperature differences that a single bulk-temperature estimate misses.</p><p>I built this independent thermal screening example to examine a series cooling architecture: two cold plates cool opposite faces, while the second plate receives water already warmed by the first. It illustrates why coolant temperature alone does not establish the hottest module temperature, and why transient heat storage must be included when interpreting cooling performance.</p><p>This is a Khani Solutions portfolio demonstration using assumed materials and operating conditions. It is not proprietary Wisk or Boeing hardware, an employer analysis, or a validated aircraft battery design. It is an operating-load cooling model, not a thermal-runaway initiation, propagation or exhaust calculation.</p><h2>Workflow, model and results</h2>'''
BATTERY_END = '''<h2>What to do with these results</h2><p>Use this example to frame architecture and test questions: whether a parallel coolant arrangement changes temperature uniformity, how flow rate affects heat removal and pumping demand, and how interface resistance or cell-to-cell heat variation affects the hotspot. Those variations and defects were not calculated in this run. The imposed 300 W load remains an input, rather than a prediction of electrochemical heat generation.</p><p>Design qualification would require representative material and interface measurements, adequate flow resolution, turbulence sensitivity where appropriate, mesh and time-step convergence, and comparison with module and circuit test data. A relatively cool outlet is not proof of a cool module core. A one-hour transient result is not a demonstrated steady operating limit.</p><h2>Connection to the external plume study</h2><p>Cooling and venting belong to different parts of battery thermal engineering. This model follows heat conduction and coolant transport in a module assembly. The aircraft plume study follows a hypothetical external hot-gas source after discharge. The two demonstrations share a focus on thermal-fluid behavior but are not coupled and do not establish thermal-runaway prevention.</p>'''

def study_page(intro_html, title, summary, media, text, other_url, other_title):
    return intro_html('Independent engineering case study', title, summary) + f'''<section class="wrap case-study"><p class="study-note">By Mohammadreza Khani, PhD · Khani Solutions · Illustrative numerical simulation</p>{media}<p class="quiet">The video is a presentation of solver results. Assumptions, numerical checks and interpretation are documented below.</p>{details(text)}<div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/AISolutions/experience.html#wisk-experience">Back to Wisk experience &amp; demonstration videos</a><a class="button secondary" href="{other_url}">{other_title} →</a><a class="text-link" href="/AISolutions/simulations.html">All analysis &amp; modeling →</a></div></section>'''
